<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Komi peoples</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Komi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Komi are a Finno-Ugric people of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-russia">European Russia</a>, divided mainly into the Komi-Zyrians of the Komi Republic and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/komi-permyaks">Komi-Permyaks</a> of Perm Krai. Their lands supplied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> to Novgorod and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, and in the late fourteenth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-of-perm">Stephen of Perm</a> converted many of them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and created the Old Permic script for their language.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/komi-peoples.jpg" alt="Komi peoples" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Irina Kazanskaya from Moscow, Russia · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Komi_peoples.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
